#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230107} :activity/id #uuid "e5d881c7-3c4a-52c1-9e67-d3807291b86c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048817 :topic/id #uuid "c277f404-7746-5317-a99f-359354eb8c6a" :topic/math-academy-id 6682} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230184 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^{b} f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the integration variable and $t$ is a parameter. How do we compute $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} I}{\\mathrm{d} t}$?\n\nIf $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b] \\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R,$ then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d} t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as **differentiating under the integral sign**.\n\nThe integral represents the total area under the curve $y = f(x,t).$ Changing the parameter $t$ alters the height of the curve at each $x,$ but the interval $[a,b]$ remains fixed.\nThus, the total change in area is obtained by summing (integrating) how each point changes.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} e^{-tx}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-tx}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}({e}^{-tx})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-x{e}^{-tx}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can then evaluate this integral using the usual methods. For the purposes of integration, we treat $t$ as a constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "2c1e693d-0af4-542d-87dd-e902e76bf8d7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14473 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d5bb1824-d10b-50d3-9fb2-1ad92235ca90" :step/math-academy-id 611854 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230108}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230220 :tutorial/content "Now, given that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle I(t)=\\int_{-1}^{1} (3x-t^2)^2\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nlet's calculate $I'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$ by *differentiating under the integral sign*.\n\nRecall that if $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R,$ then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute $I'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\int_{-1}^{1} (3x-t^2)^2\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\int_{-1}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(3x-t^2)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3x-{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3x-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3x-{t}^{2})\\cdot (-2t) \\\\  & =-4t(3x-{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{'}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-4t(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4t{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}-{t}^{2}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0-2{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{'}(t) & =-4t(-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5875ac89-b808-53fa-b46e-33fadfb23647" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14657 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "86e6d79d-861a-515d-864d-c0329ef14eb6" :step/math-academy-id 611855 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228907 :question/id #uuid "301e7fe6-6892-5e0a-9368-b3857ae8fa39" :question/math-academy-id "e-21649"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321052}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"} :step/id #uuid "a87fd0a0-542a-56a3-9f60-3db5c26834a8" :step/math-academy-id 611856 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230110}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230197 :tutorial/content "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\qquad t \\in [c,d].\n$$\n\nThen, *differentiation under the integral sign* applies, meaning\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\int_{a}^b \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x,\n$$\n\nprovided the following conditions are met:\n\n- $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b] \\times [c,d],$ and\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to compute $I'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (2x+t^3)^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x, \\qquad t \\in [0,5].\n$$\n\nWe may differentiate under the integral sign provided that the function $f(x,t) = (2x+t^3)^2$ fits the necessary criteria. Let's check.\n\nFix $t_0 \\in (0,5)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,5],$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\n- Notice that $f(x,t) = (2x+t^3)^2$ is continuous on $R.$\n\n- Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = 6(2x+t^3)t^2,\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$\n\nThus, the operation is valid. We obtain\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} 6(2x+t^3)t^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "615f6015-058c-5a82-9f79-7f3c62982c3f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14538 :tutorial/title "Checking the Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "1469ff2b-3ce2-5cf7-a237-aea623f3e876" :step/math-academy-id 611859 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228922 :question/id #uuid "830924ac-a51c-505a-b5b4-c8a4ad916dfe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311558}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"} :step/id #uuid "085c51cb-93b7-5cdd-ba82-cae0f0193721" :step/math-academy-id 611860}] :activity/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229252}]}
